  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "Using a statistically validated bipartite network framework on 51 million grocery receipts from 95 Danish municipalities, this study isolates food co-purchasing patterns that extend beyond individual product popularity. The resulting modular dietary communities explain 25% to 50% of the remaining variance in cardiometabolic prescription rates after controlling for sociodemographics, whereas ultra-processed food prevalence shows no spatial variation and sits at the network periphery.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that geographic health disparities are driven by how foods are systematically paired in baskets rather than just the isolated consumption of specific items, offering a scalable method for spatial nutritional epidemiology."
